<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Revolution was the period of upheaval in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that began with the meeting of the Estates-General in 1789, abolished the monarchy in 1792 and ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s seizure of power in 1799. Its ideas of popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, equality and the rights of man spread far beyond France, inspiring settlers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graaff-reinet">Graaff-Reinet</a> and Swellendam, while the wars it began reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and its colonies for a generation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolution.jpg" alt="French Revolution" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Between 1715 and 1789 the French population grew from 21 to 28 million, and the middle class tripled in size. The benefits of rising prosperity were largely restricted to the rentier and mercantile classes, while living standards fell for wage labourers and peasant farmers who rented their land. An economic recession from 1785 and bad harvests in 1787 and 1788 led to high unemployment and food prices.</p><p>The state's finances were in crisis. The nobility and the Church benefited from exemptions, so the tax burden fell mainly on the lower classes, and the parlements could block new tax laws. By 1788 half of state revenue went on servicing the debt. Reforms proposed by the finance minister Calonne in 1786 were rejected, and the notables and parlements argued that new taxes could be approved only by an Estates-General, last held in 1614.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Estates General met in May 1789, and in June the representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> re-constituted themselves as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>. On 14 July a crowd, joined by soldiers, attacked the Bastille, a royal fortress with large stores of arms. Its governor surrendered after fighting that cost the lives of 83 attackers. The Bastille held only seven prisoners but was a potent symbol of the Ancien Régime. The Assembly then abolished feudalism, placed the Catholic Church under state control and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a>.</p><p>Military defeats after the outbreak of war in April 1792 led to the insurrection of 10 August 1792. The monarchy was replaced by the First Republic in September, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> was executed in January 1793. Political power then passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>, dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobin-club">Jacobins</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a>. About 16,000 people were sentenced by the Revolutionary Tribunal and executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror">Reign of Terror</a>, which ended in July 1794.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Directory replaced the Committee in November 1795 but faced internal unrest, a weak economy and an expensive war. It ended with the coup of 18 Brumaire on 9 November 1799 and the Consulate, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> as First Consul. The Revolution also began the French Revolutionary Wars, which lasted until Napoleon's defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> in 1815, involved every major European power and redrew the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>Historians disagree about the role of ideology: Jonathan Israel holds that the radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> was the primary driving force, while Cobban argues that the revolutionaries mostly sought practical solutions to immediate problems.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>French Revolution</b> the Republic refused in 1793 to accept the quèstia, rejecting the French co-sovereignty as a feudal relic, and for over a decade the bishop was the only co-prince until Napoleon restored the French rights in 1806 at the Andorrans' request.</blockquote><a href="/empire/andorra">Andorra · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Norman customary law was written down around 1200 in the Très ancien coutumier and later in the Grand coutumier de Normandie, which in revised form remained the law of the province until the <b>French Revolution</b> and still underlies law in the Channel Islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Inspired by the <b>French Revolution</b>, the Society of United Irishmen led a rebellion in 1798, supported by a small French landing in County Mayo; it was suppressed with heavy loss of life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Inspired by the <b>French Revolution</b>, the Society of United Irishmen sought a republic uniting Catholics, Protestants and Dissenters, and Wolfe Tone obtained French support; a French fleet reached Bantry Bay in December 1796 but storms prevented a landing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferdinand IV, who succeeded in 1759 when his father Charles left to become king of Spain, and his wife Maria Carolina of Austria, sister of Marie Antoinette, turned against reformers after the <b>French Revolution</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>French Revolution</b> brought the crisis to a head.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
